<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merseburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Merseburg is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It was a royal palace of the Ottonian kings, and Otto I founded a bishopric there in 968; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Thietmar wrote his famous chronicle there, and the cathedral library holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg-charms">Merseburg Charms</a>. It was later the seat of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-merseburg">Saxe-Merseburg</a> and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merseburg.jpg" alt="Merseburg" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jwaller · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MerseburgDomschlo%C3%9Fblick.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Otto I founded a monastery at Magdeburg in 937 and, after years of negotiation with the Pope and the Archbishop of Mainz, raised it to an archbishopric in 968, with suffragans at <b>Merseburg</b>, Meissen, Zeitz (later Naumburg), Brandenburg and Havelberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 1656, Christian I received <b>Merseburg</b>, the secularised bishopric and its district, founding the Duchy of Saxe-Merseburg, which also held parts of Lusatia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-merseburg">Duchy of Saxe-Merseburg</a></li><li><blockquote>When Gero died in 965 Otto I divided his march into smaller units: the Northern March, the Margraviate of Meissen, the March of Lusatia (later Margraviate of Lower Lusatia), and the marches of <b>Merseburg</b> and Zeitz.</blockquote><a href="/state/marca-geronis">Marca Geronis</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto I founded the bishopric of <b>Merseburg</b> in 968 to fulfil a vow made before the Battle of Lechfeld, as a suffragan of Magdeburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-merseburg">Prince-Bishopric of Merseburg</a></li></ul></section>
